    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A confederation of north German peoples between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, ruled by nobles and assemblies rather than kings.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> sea raiders troubled Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, and Saxon settlers helped create <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon England</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxon-wars">Saxon Wars</a> of 772–804 ended their independence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportations</a> and forced conversion.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In Finnish and Estonian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> is still named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>: Saksa and Saksamaa.</p></aside>
    </section>

